","known_bad_label":"Whitespace-only malformed statements are incorrectly accepted and delivered through batch callbacks."},{"id":"cap-v2-callbacks_and_events-java-repair-02","problem":"Repair the implementation below.

`replay` synchronously interprets each element of `records` as an encoded property event. A valid record has the form `key=value`, where `key` contains at least one character. The value may be empty and may contain additional `=` characters. Split a valid record only at its first `=` and call `listener.onEntry(key, value)`.

A `null` record, a record without `=`, or a record whose first character is `=` is malformed. For each such record, call `listener.onMalformed(index, record)` instead of `onEntry`. Process records in input order. After every listener callback has returned, call `completion.onComplete(validCount, malformedCount)` exactly once.

You may assume all arrays and callbacks are non-null. Callbacks execute synchronously and do not throw exceptions.

Return a corrected complete `Solution.java` source file using this exact API:

```java
public static void replay(String[] records, EntryListener listener,
                          Completion completion)
```

Known-bad source:

```java
public final class Solution {
    private Solution() {}

    public interface EntryListener {
        void onEntry(String key, String value);
        void onMalformed(int index, String record);
    }

    public interface Completion {
        void onComplete(int validCount, int malformedCount);
    }

    public static void replay(String[] records, EntryListener listener,
                              Completion completion) {
        int valid = 0;
        int malformed = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < records.length; i++) {
            String record = records[i];
            int separator = record == null ? -1 : record.indexOf('=');
            if (separator < 0) {
                listener.onMalformed(i, record);
                malformed++;
                continue;
            }

            listener.onEntry(record.substring(0, separator),
                             record.substring(separator + 1));
            valid++;
        }

        completion.onComplete(valid, malformed);
    }
}
