","known_bad_label":"Direct dependencies are traversed in caller-provided list order instead of the required stable lexicographic order."},{"id":"cap-v2-graph_and_tree-java-generation-02","problem":"Create a complete `Solution.java` implementation for this exact API:

```java
public static List<String> hierarchyPaths(
        Map<String, List<String>> children, String root)
```

`children` describes a rooted tree whose node names are unique. Return the path of every node in a preorder traversal. A path consists of node names joined with `/`; the root's path is just the root name. For example, a node named `leaf` below `root` and then `branch` has path `root/branch/leaf`.

The result must have stable ordering independent of the map and child-list iteration order. At every node, visit its direct children in ascending lexicographic order, recursively completing each child's subtree before visiting the next sibling.

You may assume `children` and `root` are non-null, every node is a key in the map, every child list is non-null, the structure is a tree rooted at `root`, and node names are non-empty and contain no `/`. Use only the Java standard library.","reference_code":"import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public final class Solution {
    private Solution() {}

    public static List<String> hierarchyPaths(
            Map<String, List<String>> children, String root) {
        List<String> paths = new ArrayList<>();
        collect(root, root, children, paths);
        return paths;
    }

    private static void collect(String node, String path,
                                Map<String, List<String>> children,
                                List<String> paths) {
        paths.add(path);
        List<String> orderedChildren = new ArrayList<>(children.get(node));
        orderedChildren.sort(String::compareTo);
        for (String child : orderedChildren) {
            collect(child, path + "/" + child, children, paths);
        }
    }
}
","known_bad_code":"import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public final class Solution {
    private Solution() {}

    public static List<String> hierarchyPaths(
            Map<String, List<String>> children, String root) {
        List<String> paths = new ArrayList<>();
        collect(root, root, children, paths);
        paths.sort(String::compareTo);
        return paths;
    }

    private static void collect(String node, String path,
                                Map<String, List<String>> children,
                                List<String> paths) {
        paths.add(path);
        for (String child : children.get(node)) {
            collect(child, path + "/" + child, children, paths);
        }
    }
}
","known_bad_label":"Sorting completed path strings does not implement the required preorder with lexicographically ordered siblings."},{"id":"cap-v2-graph_and_tree-java-repair-01","problem":"Repair the implementation below.

`reachable` traverses a directed graph and returns a set containing `start` and every node reachable from it by following zero or more outgoing edges. Cycles may be present. Each invocation must be isolated: its result and traversal state must depend only on the graph and start node passed to that invocation, even when `reachable` is called repeatedly in the same JVM.

You may assume `graph` and `start` are non-null, every referenced node is a key in `graph`, and every adjacency list is non-null. No iteration order is required for the returned set.

Return a corrected complete `Solution.java` source file using this exact API:

```java
public static Set<String> reachable(
        Map<String, List<String>> graph, String start)
```

Known-bad source:

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Queue;
import java.util.Set;

public final class Solution {
    private static final Set<String> VISITED = new HashSet<>();

    private Solution() {}

    public static Set<String> reachable(
            Map<String, List<String>> graph, String start) {
        Queue<String> pending = new ArrayDeque<>();
        if (VISITED.add(start)) {
            pending.add(start);
        }

        while (!pending.isEmpty()) {
            String node = pending.remove();
            for (String next : graph.get(node)) {
                if (VISITED.add(next)) {
                    pending.add(next);
                }
            }
        }

        return new HashSet<>(VISITED);
    }
}
